  "details": "I am a Japanese user eager to participate in the VRChat Creator Economy by selling avatars and other content. However, I have some concerns about the current setup that I hope you can address to make the platform more accessible and inclusive for international users like me.\n\nEnglish-Only Interface: The payment process and registration of personal information through Tilia are only available in English. As a non-native English speaker, this feels challenging and intimidating, especially when handling sensitive information like identification and payment details. Adding Japanese language support for Tilia’s interface, terms of service, and support documentation would greatly enhance accessibility and user confidence.\n\nUS-Centric Legal Framework: Tilia operates under US law, which feels unfamiliar and concerning for Japanese users. Compliance with Japanese laws, such as the Act on the Protection of Personal Information, would make the platform feel safer and more trustworthy. Ideally, the Creator Economy should support localized legal frameworks to accommodate creators from various countries.\n\nLimited Payment Options: Currently, Tilia only supports PayPal for payouts, which may not be ideal for all users. Offering alternative payment methods that comply with local laws and are widely used in Japan (e.g., bank transfers or other local payment systems) would make the platform more inclusive. If possible, introducing payment processors other than Tilia would further improve flexibility and trust for international creators.\n\nI understand that implementing these changes may require time and resources, but I believe that adding multilingual support, region-specific compliance, and diverse payment options would encourage more creators from Japan and other countries to engage with the Creator Economy. Thank you for your dedication to building such an inspiring platform, and I hope you consider these suggestions to make VRChat even more welcoming for global creators.\n\nI am using a translation software, so I am sorry if the nuance is wrong.",
